   "source": [
    "# Libra Interactive Demo: Machine Learning in One Line of Code
    "
    "Welcome to the comprehensive Libra demo! This notebook showcases how Libra enables you to create powerful machine learning models with just one line of code.
    "
    "## What is Libra?
    "
    "Libra is a Python library that automates the machine learning process, allowing you to:
    "- Build ML models in one line of code
    "- Automatically handle data preprocessing
    "- Perform automated model selection
    "- Create neural networks effortlessly
    "- Generate predictions with minimal setup
    "
    "Let's explore Libra's capabilities through practical examples!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Setup and Installation
    "
    "First, let's install the required packages and import necessary libraries."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Install required packages (uncomment if needed)
    "# !pip install libra matplotlib pandas numpy seaborn scikit-learn
    "
    "# Import libraries
    "import pandas as pd
    "import numpy as np
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
    "import seaborn as sns
    "from sklearn.datasets import make_regression, make_classification, load_iris, load_boston
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
    "import warnings
    "warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')
    "
    "# Import Libra
    "from libra import client
    "
    "print(\"✅ All libraries imported successfully!\")
    "print(\"🚀 Ready to explore Libra's one-line machine learning capabilities!\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Example 1: Regression with Libra
    "
    "Let's start with a regression problem. We'll predict house prices using a synthetic dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Create synthetic regression dataset
    "X_reg, y_reg = make_regression(n_samples=1000, n_features=10, noise=0.1, random_state=42)
    "feature_names = [f'feature_{i+1}' for i in range(10)]
    "reg_df = pd.DataFrame(X_reg, columns=feature_names)
    "reg_df['target'] = y_reg
    "
    "print(\"📊 Regression Dataset Created:\")
    "print(f\"Shape: {reg_df.shape}\")
    "print(\"\
    "print(reg_df.head())
    "
    "# Visualize the target distribution
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))
    "plt.subplot(1, 2, 1)
    "plt.hist(reg_df['target'], bins=30, alpha=0.7, color='skyblue')
    "plt.title('Target Distribution')
    "plt.xlabel('Target Value')
    "plt.ylabel('Frequency')
    "
    "plt.subplot(1, 2, 2)
    "plt.scatter(reg_df['feature_1'], reg_df['target'], alpha=0.6, color='coral')
    "plt.title('Feature 1 vs Target')
    "plt.xlabel('Feature 1')
    "plt.ylabel('Target')
    "
    "plt.tight_layout()
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Save dataset for Libra
    "reg_df.to_csv('regression_data.csv', index=False)
    "
    "# 🎯 ONE LINE OF CODE: Train a regression model with Libra!
    "newClient = client('regression_data.csv')
    "newClient.regression()
    "
    "print(\"🎉 Regression model trained successfully with just one line of code!\")
    "print(\"📈 Libra automatically:\")
    "print(\"   • Loaded and preprocessed the data\")
    "print(\"   • Selected the best algorithm\")
    "print(\"   • Trained and evaluated the model\")
    "print(\"   • Generated performance metrics\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Example 2: Classification with Libra
    "
    "Now let's tackle a classification problem using the famous Iris dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Load the Iris dataset
    "iris = load_iris()
    "iris_df = pd.DataFrame(iris.data, columns=iris.feature_names)
    "iris_df['species'] = iris.target_names[iris.target]
    "
    "print(\"🌸 Iris Classification Dataset:\")
    "print(f\"Shape: {iris_df.shape}\")
    "print(\"\
    "print(iris_df.head())
    "print(\"\
    "print(iris_df['species'].value_counts())
    "
    "# Visualize the dataset
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 4))
    "
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 1)
    "sns.scatterplot(data=iris_df, x='sepal length (cm)', y='sepal width (cm)', hue='species')
    "plt.title('Sepal Dimensions')
    "
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 2)
    "sns.scatterplot(data=iris_df, x='petal length (cm)', y='petal width (cm)', hue='species')
    "plt.title('Petal Dimensions')
    "
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 3)
    "iris_df['species'].value_counts().plot(kind='bar', color=['lightcoral', 'lightblue', 'lightgreen'])
    "plt.title('Species Distribution')
    "plt.xticks(rotation=45)
    "
    "plt.tight_layout()
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Save dataset for Libra
    "iris_df.to_csv('iris_data.csv', index=False)
    "
    "# 🎯 ONE LINE OF CODE: Train a classification model with Libra!
    "iris_client = client('iris_data.csv')
    "iris_client.classification(target='species')
    "
    "print(\"🎉 Classification model trained successfully with just one line of code!\")
    "print(\"🔍 Libra automatically:\")
    "print(\"   • Handled categorical target encoding\")
    "print(\"   • Performed feature scaling\")
    "print(\"   • Selected optimal classification algorithm\")
    "print(\"   • Generated confusion matrix and metrics\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Example 3: Neural Networks with Libra
    "
    "Let's create a neural network for a more complex classification task."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Create a more complex classification dataset
    "X_complex, y_complex = make_classification(
    "    n_samples=2000, 
    "    n_features=20, 
    "    n_informative=15, 
    "    n_redundant=5, 
    "    n_classes=3, 
    "    random_state=42
    ")
    "
    "feature_names = [f'feature_{i+1}' for i in range(20)]
    "complex_df = pd.DataFrame(X_complex, columns=feature_names)
    "complex_df['class'] = y_complex
    "
    "print(\"🧠 Complex Neural Network Dataset:\")
    "print(f\"Shape: {complex_df.shape}\")
    "print(f\"Classes: {sorted(complex_df['class'].unique())}\")
    "print(\"\
    "print(complex_df['class'].value_counts().sort_index())
    "
    "# Visualize feature correlations
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))
    "
    "plt.subplot(1, 2, 1)
    "correlation_matrix = complex_df[feature_names[:10]].corr()
    "sns.heatmap(correlation_matrix, annot=False, cmap='coolwarm', center=0)
    "plt.title('Feature Correlations (First 10 Features)')
    "
    "plt.subplot(1, 2, 2)
    "complex_df['class'].value_counts().sort_index().plot(kind='bar', color=['#FF6B6B', '#4ECDC4', '#45B7D1'])
    "plt.title('Class Distribution')
    "plt.xlabel('Class')
    "plt.ylabel('Count')
    "plt.xticks(rotation=0)
    "
    "plt.tight_layout()
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Save dataset for Libra
    "complex_df.to_csv('neural_network_data.csv', index=False)
    "
    "# 🎯 ONE LINE OF CODE: Create a neural network with Libra!
    "nn_client = client('neural_network_data.csv')
    "nn_client.neural_network(target='class')
    "
    "print(\"🎉 Neural network created and trained successfully with just one line of code!\")
    "print(\"🧠 Libra automatically:\")
    "print(\"   • Designed optimal network architecture\")
    "print(\"   • Selected appropriate activation functions\")
    "print(\"   • Configured learning rate and optimization\")
    "print(\"   • Trained with early stopping and validation\")
    "print(\"   • Generated training history and metrics\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
